::GEO_U04_UA_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation changes size while preserving angle measures? {
=A. Dilation#A dilation scales lengths but preserves angle measures, so it supports similarity.
~B. Translation#A translation preserves size and shape.
~C. Reflection#A reflection preserves size and shape.
~D. Rotation#A rotation preserves size and shape. T
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Two triangles have &angle;A &cong; &angle;D and &angle;B &cong; &angle;E. Which criterion justifies similarity? {
~A. SSS similarity#SSS similarity requires all three side ratios.
=B. AA similarity#Two congruent angle pairs are enough to prove triangle similarity.
~C. SAS similarity#SAS similarity requires two proportional side pairs and the included angle.
~D. HL congruence#HL proves congruence in right triangles, not similarity here. T
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Two similar figures have corresponding sides 5 and 20. What is the scale factor from first to second? {
~A. 15#This subtracts instead of comparing by ratio.
~B. 25#This adds the side lengths.
=C. 4#20 divided by 5 equals 4.
~D. &frac14;#This is the reverse scale factor. T
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 3 and 4. What is the hypotenuse? {
~A. 7#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~B. 1#Subtracting the legs does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
~C. 12#Multiplying the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
=D. 5#Use a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>; the square root of the sum is 5.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 5 and 12. What is the hypotenuse? {
=A. 13#Use a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>; the square root of the sum is 13.
~B. 17#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~C. 7#Subtracting the legs does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
~D. 60#Multiplying the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 8 and 15. What is the hypotenuse? {
~A. 23#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
=B. 17#Use a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>; the square root of the sum is 17.
~C. 7#Subtracting the legs does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
~D. 120#Multiplying the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 7 and 24. What is the hypotenuse? {
~A. 31#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~B. 17#Subtracting the legs does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
=C. 25#Use a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>; the square root of the sum is 25.
~D. 168#Multiplying the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 9 and 40. What is the hypotenuse? {
~A. 49#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~B. 31#Subtracting the legs does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
~C. 360#Multiplying the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
=D. 41#Use a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>; the square root of the sum is 41.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 12 and 16. What is the hypotenuse? {
=A. 20#Use a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>; the square root of the sum is 20.
~B. 28#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~C. 4#Subtracting the legs does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
~D. 192#Multiplying the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which ratio defines sine? {
~A. <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This defines cosine.
=B. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#Sine compares the opposite side to the hypotenuse.
~C. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#This defines tangent.
~D. <sup>hypotenuse</sup>&frasl;<sub>opposite</sub>#This reverses sine. T
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A ramp forms a right triangle with legs 6 ft and 8 ft. What is the hypotenuse length? {
~A. 14 ft#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~B. 2 ft#Subtracting the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
=C. 10 ft#Use the Pythagorean Theorem; the hypotenuse is 10 ft.
~D. 48 ft#Multiplying the legs does not find a side length.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A support beam forms a right triangle with legs 9 ft and 12 ft. What is the hypotenuse length? {
~A. 21 ft#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~B. 3 ft#Subtracting the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~C. 108 ft#Multiplying the legs does not find a side length.
=D. 15 ft#Use the Pythagorean Theorem; the hypotenuse is 15 ft.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A ladder brace forms a right triangle with legs 12 ft and 16 ft. What is the hypotenuse length? {
=A. 20 ft#Use the Pythagorean Theorem; the hypotenuse is 20 ft.
~B. 28 ft#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~C. 4 ft#Subtracting the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~D. 192 ft#Multiplying the legs does not find a side length.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A diagonal walkway forms a right triangle with legs 15 ft and 20 ft. What is the hypotenuse length? {
